WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.820
Now before we jump in to using grooms inside of the editor,

2
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:07.459
I want us to take a quick overview look

3
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:10.900
of what grooms actually are so that we kind of understand

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.179
the underlying tech a little bit better,

5
00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:15.919
as this will help us make better grooms

6
00:00:15.919 --> 00:00:18.420
that fit our visual needs.

7
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.260
Now, when I talk about groom or the groom system,

8
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.379
this is a system of tools that allow you

9
00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:28.739
to significantly improve the visual fidelity

10
00:00:28.739 --> 00:00:32.420
of your hair simulations, and that

11
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.179
is accomplished by actually simulating

12
00:00:36.179 --> 00:00:41.020
every individual strand of hair that you have on your head.

13
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:41.740
That is right.

14
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:42.740
You heard that correct.

15
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.020
When you're utilizing groom inside of Unreal Engine,

16
00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.539
what you're actually doing is you

17
00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:51.219
are importing a series of splines that

18
00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:57.829
are used to then simulate hair and even fur or feathers.

19
00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:59.869
We can see this example right here.

20
00:00:59.869 --> 00:01:03.030
We have a head that each one of these little individual hairs

21
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.469
you see here are a groom, and then we also

22
00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:09.200
have these feathers which act as a groom as well.

23
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.879
Now one thing to note that groom inside of Unreal

24
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:19.959
is utilized for displaying and optimizing as well as adjusting

25
00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:21.480
materials.

26
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.519
If you want to do something to the hair that

27
00:01:24.519 --> 00:01:28.079
involves maybe shaping it or changing it or growing it

28
00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:29.519
or something like that, that's going

29
00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:31.319
to be done in your external modeling

30
00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:35.480
tool or your digital content creation tool, your DCC,

31
00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.280
using something like XGen within Maya, Omnitrax, Yeti,

32
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.439
or shave and a haircut, or even Houdini.

33
00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:43.159
So you'll use one of those DCCs

34
00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.120
to actually generate your groom assets

35
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.640
and then import that into Unreal,

36
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.840
where you can then add physics to it

37
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.239
and adjust its lighting and material settings.

38
00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:57.840
Now if you are, again, brand new

39
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.159
or you'd like to take a look at some grooms in action,

40
00:02:01.159 --> 00:02:04.000
the following Unreal Engine 5 samples

41
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.680
all contain groom examples.

42
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.240
Now we have Valley of the Ancients,

43
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.919
which is one of the first Unreal 5 samples that

44
00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.000
was released.

45
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.960
And while this was heavily using Nanite and Lumen,

46
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.599
it also utilizes groom on the echo character.

47
00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:23.000
Taking that one step further, the sleigh animation sample

48
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.039
actually shows you how groom could

49
00:02:25.039 --> 00:02:27.479
be used in the context of a linear animation.

50
00:02:27.479 --> 00:02:28.840
In this sample, what we're doing

51
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:33.520
is we're taking the Windwalker echo character

52
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.080
and then making some linear content with it,

53
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.240
which focuses mainly on the character.

54
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.879
And by looking at this example, you

55
00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:45.400
can see a great use of groom inside of linear content.

56
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.159
And again, with some more linear content,

57
00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:48.840
there is the Meerkat demo, which

58
00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.319
Weta Digital was building to investigate

59
00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:56.319
how far they can push linear content, especially when

60
00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:58.960
it comes to fur and feathers.

61
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.960
And with this example, you can really

62
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.400
see how far you can push fur and feathers in real time.

63
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.360
Last but not least, the metahumans, all metahumans

64
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.969
utilize grooms for their hair.

65
00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:15.129
So if you open up the metahumans creator

66
00:03:15.129 --> 00:03:17.210
and you take a look at their hair or their beards

67
00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.610
or their eyelashes, all of that

68
00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.400
is going to be done inside of groom.

69
00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.960
Now, if you're kind of still wondering,

70
00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.960
well, you say working in groom and your digital content

71
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.319
creation and coloring, bringing it over into Unreal,

72
00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:31.479
what does that really mean?

73
00:03:31.479 --> 00:03:33.400
Well, what we're going to be doing in our DCC

74
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.800
again is we're going to do our creation, our look,

75
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.759
and our styling.

76
00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:42.719
So if we want our hair to maybe have like an updo

77
00:03:42.719 --> 00:03:46.439
or we want some pieces that are flying off to the side

78
00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:48.479
or we have a ponytail or some short hair,

79
00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.319
we're going to do all of that look and styling and creation

80
00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:53.599
all in our digital content creation tool,

81
00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:57.759
utilizing whatever tool for hair and fur that we want.

82
00:03:57.759 --> 00:03:59.280
Once we're done and we're ready,

83
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.919
we're going to export that as an Alembic file or a .abc

84
00:04:02.919 --> 00:04:04.919
and then bring that into Unreal Engine 5.

85
00:04:04.919 --> 00:04:06.520
Once inside of Unreal Engine 5, we're

86
00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.319
going to do things like the coloring, the rendering,

87
00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:11.520
and the physics of the hair, as well as

88
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.360
optimize it by adding level of details and things like that.

89
00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:18.519
So again, when we are dealing with our groom,

90
00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.040
we're going to be doing a majority of the creation

91
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.839
and the editing inside of our DCC.

92
00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:25.959
But once we're done with that, all of the visual updates

93
00:04:25.959 --> 00:04:29.079
will then happen in Unreal Engine 5.

94
00:04:29.079 --> 00:04:32.439
So now that we know a little bit more about groom

95
00:04:32.439 --> 00:04:35.680
and what it does and how it works at a very high level,

96
00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:38.000
next we're going to take a look at enabling the groom

97
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.720
plugin so that we can actually visualize our grooms

98
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.680
inside of Unreal Engine 5.
